  "analysis": "Comparing candidate platforms against the selection criteria reveals three distinct architectural tradeoffs. DeepAgents (source_001) offers a LangGraph foundation, which provides a mature graph-based orchestration model with strong ecosystem support and predictable execution semantics. This makes it well-suited for complex multi-step agent workflows where control flow must be explicit and auditable. However, its generality means teams must invest in custom validation scaffolding, since the platform itself does not enforce a separation between development and production-grade validation. DeepSeek Harness (source_004) takes the opposite approach: it is explicitly reserved for the outer validation environment, meaning it is not intended as a general-purpose development platform but as a gatekeeper for final quality assurance. This yields strong guarantees about what reaches production, but it creates a two-platform operational burden and may slow iteration because developers cannot use the same tooling end-to-end. The benchmark design (source_007) introduces a third consideration: any selected platform must expose quality, cost, and critical-path latency as first-class observable metrics. Platforms that do not natively instrument these dimensions will require additional telemetry work, eroding the cost advantage of otherwise cheaper options. The core tradeoff is therefore between orchestration flexibility (DeepAgents), validation rigor (DSH), and observability readiness (benchmark alignment). A combined approach—using DeepAgents for agent construction and DSH for outer validation—mitigates individual weaknesses but increases integration complexity and operational overhead. Teams prioritizing speed of development will favor DeepAgents alone; teams with strict compliance requirements will favor the DSH gate; teams needing defensible ROI reporting must ensure benchmark metric coverage regardless of platform choice.",
